"""The app-started job slice (`docs/design/agent-jobs.md`), driven through a
REAL subprocess exactly as `test_agent_api.py` drives the chat `ask()` flow --
`AgentSettings.executable` points at the same `fake_agent_cli.py`, so these
tests prove `jobs.py`'s actual argv, environment, persistence and fan-out
rather than a mock standing in for the claim that they work.

Two things do NOT mirror `test_agent_api.py`. First: a job's rows are written
through `get_session_factory()` (the app's real, engine-bound sessionmaker),
never the `session` fixture's rollback-wrapped connection -- deliberately,
the same way `jobs.py`'s own module docstring explains it: a job must outlive
the one request that reads it back, and a connection whose every write rolls
back at test teardown cannot hold state across the several calls these tests
make. Second: `AgentJob`/`AgentJobEvent` are `WorkspaceScoped`, so a job needs
an actually-COMMITTED workspace to satisfy its own foreign key -- `_provision`
below replicates `get_identity`'s own upsert chain directly against a real
session, since the ordinary `client`/`session` fixture's auto-provisioning
lives inside a transaction that is rolled back at teardown and was never
visible to the separate connection `jobs.py` writes through in the first
place. Job and workspace rows this file commits are real; they are not
cleaned up, the same way `test_workspaces_api.py`'s own stable-uid fixtures
are not -- each test's own fresh `uuid4()` job id keeps them from colliding.
"""

